ngle July D-sponsored poll showed a 42-42 tie"], "rationale": "The seat's R+7 lean, an open-seat Republican who just survived a hard primary with overwhelming financial resources, and a Solid-to-Likely R rating consensus point to a comfortable Republican win. The lone tied poll is old and Democratic-sponsored, so it warrants only a modest nudge toward the Democrat.", "watch": "Whether Strada's divisive recount-tested primary leaves lingering GOP fractures, or a national environment shifts further toward Democrats, could tighten the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 62 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Open seat: Daniel Webster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 11th District is an open Republican-held seat after Daniel Webster's retirement, with a partisan lean of about seven points toward the GOP. Joe Strada won a razor-thin Republican primary that went to a recount, but enters the general election with a dominant financial position, having raised over $5 million against roughly $120,000 for Democrat James Pericola. A Libertarian is also on the ballot.\n\nRatings run from Solid R to Likely R, and prediction markets give Democrats about a 12% chance. The only public poll, a July survey from a Democratic-leaning firm, showed a 42-42 tie, but its age and sponsorship limit its weight. The fundamentals and money advantage point to a Republican win in the high single digits, with modest residual uncertainty from the sparse polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1071, "margin": -7.48}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1312, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1199, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0703, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0839, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0989, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1239, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0883, "margin": -9.0}]}